! v0.73.0 PCM test 4:  fluoride anion F- in water (HF/STO-3G, C-PCM).
!
! Single-atom CHARGED solute.  This is the cleanest possible Gauss-
! law check: for a net charge Q sitting fully inside the cavity, the
! total apparent surface charge satisfies
!
!     sum(q) = -Q * (eps - 1) / eps
!
! exactly in the continuum limit.  For F- (Q = -1) in water
! (eps = 78.36, f = 0.987), the prediction is
!
!     sum(q) = -(-1) * 0.987 = +0.987
!
! The discretized sum will differ from this by terms of order
! (1/N_tess) and (a/R)^L for partial-wave moments above L = 0;
! agreement to within ~1 % at lebedev_n = 110 is a good sanity
! check on the cavity discretization and the C-PCM solver.
!
! Solvation free energy is also expected to be large and negative
! (small ions in water are deeply stabilized): rough estimate from
! Born model G_solv ~ -Q^2 / (2 R_F) * f, with R_F ~ 1.47 A * 1.20 =
! 1.764 A, so G_solv ~ -0.157 Ha = -98.5 kcal/mol.  HF/STO-3G with
! Bondi radii will overestimate that magnitude considerably.
%method  HF
%basis   sto-3g
%charge  -1
%mult    1
%job     energy
%pcm     cpcm     ! v0.74+ default is iefpcm; v0.73 tests pin cpcm explicitly
%solvent water
%geom angstrom
F  0.000000   0.000000   0.000000
*end
